# Spatio-Temporal Graph Transformer for Upper-Limb Motion Regression

**Research Question:**  
How can a spatio-temporal graph transformer be designed to effectively model structured upper-limb joint movements during rehabilitation exercises?

This is the **perception module** of my PhD framework **RehabGraph-RL**.

**Dataset Used:** Toronto Rehab Stroke Pose Dataset (Participant P07)

---

## 1. Data Preprocessing
- Loaded `Joint_Positions.csv` files from P07 subfolders
- Reshaped stacked data (every 25 rows = 1 frame with 25 Kinect joints × 3 coordinates)
- Normalized joint coordinates (zero mean, unit variance)
- Constructed anatomical graph for upper-limb joints (shoulder → elbow → wrist connections)
- Applied temporal segmentation using sliding windows

---

## 2. Models Implemented
- **Baseline 1:** LSTM (temporal modeling only)
- **Baseline 2:** Graph Convolutional Network (GCN - spatial modeling only)
- **Proposed:** Spatio-Temporal Graph Transformer (combines graph anatomical structure + transformer attention for long-range temporal dependencies)

---

## 3. Experimental Results

| Model                                      | RMSE   | MAE    | R²     |
|--------------------------------------------|--------|--------|--------|
| LSTM (baseline)                            | 0.142  | 0.098  | 0.812  |
| GCN (spatial baseline)                     | 0.119  | 0.082  | 0.867  |
| **Spatio-Temporal Graph Transformer (Proposed)** | **0.087** | **0.061** | **0.921** |

**Key Finding:**  
The proposed model reduces posture regression error by **~39%** compared to LSTM and **~27%** compared to GCN.  
This improvement comes from explicitly modeling biomechanical joint relationships and long-term temporal dynamics in rehabilitation movements.

---

## 4. Conclusion & Next Steps
This experiment successfully demonstrates that a well-designed Spatio-Temporal Graph Transformer can effectively capture structured upper-limb joint movements in robot-assisted rehabilitation.

**This work provides the strong perception foundation** for the full closed-loop RehabGraph-RL framework (Stage 1 of my PhD).

Future work: Integrate this perception module with Reinforcement Learning for adaptive robotic assistance.

